## React Re-renders: Causes and Prevention

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, "change" means value equality. For objects and functions, it means **reference equality** — a new reference always triggers a re-render, even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a new function reference → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

JavaScript's `===` on objects/functions compares memory addresses, not contents. Since each render call re-executes the function body, inline literals always produce new references.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
- Use when: a child is expensive to render and its parent re-renders frequently with unchanged props.
- Don't use blindly — the comparison itself has a cost.

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a **computed value**.
- Use when: the computation is genuinely expensive (sorting large arrays, complex derivations).
- Don't use for cheap calculations — the memoization overhead isn't worth it.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a **function reference**.
- Use when: passing a callback to a `React.memo` child (otherwise memo is defeated) or as a `useEffect` dependency.
- Without `React.memo` on the child, `useCallback` alone does nothing useful.

The three work together: `React.memo` stops unnecessary renders, `useCallback`/`useMemo` stabilize the references that `memo` compares.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context **value** changes reference — even if the data inside is the same.

```jsx
// BAD: new object every render → all consumers re-render on every parent render
function App() {
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      ...
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fixes:**
- Memoize the context value with `useMemo`.
- Split contexts by update frequency — separate "static config" context from "frequently changing" context so slow consumers don't pay for fast updates.
- Co-locate state lower in the tree when possible so fewer components need the context.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every `App` keystroke because `filters` and `onSelect` are new references each time.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(largeDataset);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        filters={{ query }}              // new object every render
        onSelect={(id) => select(id)}    // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ items, filters, onSelect }) => {
  const visible = items.filter(i => i.name.includes(filters.query));
  return visible.map(i => <Row key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />);
};
```

**After** — stable references mean `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `query` actually changes, and `Row` components skip re-renders entirely when their item and handler are unchanged.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(largeDataset);

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ query }), [query]);
  const onSelect = useCallback((id) => select(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={items} filters={filters} onSelect={onSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, filters, onSelect }) => {
  const visible = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.name.includes(filters.query)),
    [items, filters]
  );
  return visible.map(i => <Row key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />);
});

const Row = React.memo(({ item, onSelect }) => (
  <div onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>{item.name}</div>
));
```

---

**Rule of thumb:** profile first (`React DevTools Profiler`), then optimize. Premature memoization adds complexity without measurable benefit — only stabilize references where you can demonstrate a real render cost.
